1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Hi, I'm Abhishek, and this is my submission for the Prerequisite Challenge.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:16,000
The project is a Solana program called the Vault, built with the Anchor framework.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:30,000
On Solana, a program is a smart contract — but it is stateless. It holds no data of its own.
All the data lives in separate accounts passed into each instruction. That is the mental model the project is built on.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,000
The Vault lets a user open a personal SOL vault, deposit funds, withdraw them, and close the vault.
There are four instructions: initialize, deposit, withdraw, and close.

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Let me walk through the accounts. The "user" is the signer wallet — it pays fees and owns the SOL.
The "vault_state" is a Program Derived Address, a PDA, with seeds "state" plus the user. It stores two bump values.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The "vault" is another PDA, with seeds "vault" plus the vault_state; this is the account that actually holds the deposited SOL.

7
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:25,000
A PDA is the important idea here. It is an address derived deterministically from seeds and the program ID, and it has no private key.
Because there is no private key, only our program can sign on its behalf — and that is what keeps the user's funds safe.

8
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The part I actually built is the extension. The challenge asked me to modify the "withdraw" instruction so that, when a user withdraws,
the program also calls — through a Cross-Program Invocation, a CPI — an external "registration" program.

9
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:52,000
That registration program records the user's GitHub username on-chain, inside an account it owns called "application_account".

10
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:08,000
So the full flow is: a user initializes their vault, deposits SOL, then withdraws. On withdrawal, the vault program returns the SOL to the user
AND makes a CPI into the registration program, passing my GitHub username, which creates the application account.

11
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Finally, close returns any leftover SOL and closes the vault state.

12
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:35,000
I deployed this to devnet and verified it: the registration account for my wallet now contains my GitHub handle,
which proves the CPI really happened.

13
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:50,000
That is the Vault program — a small but complete example of accounts, PDAs, and program composition on Solana. Thank you.
